 "drivingQuestion": "From where do we derive one offering for a leper afflicted many times?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Derived from Leviticus 14:2.",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "keritot.9b.11",
   "daf": "9b",
   "sentenceId": 10,
   "he": "מְצוֹרָע שֶׁנִּתְנַגֵּעַ נְגָעִים הַרְבֵּה כּוּ׳. מְנָלַן? דִּכְתִיב: ״זֹאת תּוֹרַת הַמְּצוֹרָע״ – תּוֹרָה אַחַת לִמְצוֹרָעִים הַרְבֵּה.",
   "en": "The mishna teaches that «a leper who was afflicted» with «several» instances of «leprosy» brings one single offering. The Gemara asks: «From where do we» derive this halakha? The Gemara answers that it is «as it is written: “This» shall be «the law of the leper» in the day of his purification” (Leviticus 14:2). The term “the law” teaches that «one law,» meaning one sacrificial offering, can suffice «for several lepers,» i.e., for several instances of leprosy in the same individual."
  }
 ],
